unchanged) hold both arms. LLR-281 states one floor and the named refusal, and TC-291's case (b) runs the shipped set. The derivation and the record are now complete -> why it is not ready: the shipped-skills obligation is still outside the row's obligation. \"Are at or above\" is a statement of fact. It sits in the Requirement, but before and outside the one `shall`, so a reader cannot tell from the cell's own grammar whether it binds or merely describes. The repository's form rule names exactly that defect: a statement of fact in normative text, where a reader cannot tell which part is binding. The one-`shall` rule exists to catch two obligations in one row, and stating the second without `shall` satisfies the rule's letter while defeating its purpose. Both clauses are one decision (the description floor, and the check that holds every skill to it), so the remedy is not a split. It is one `shall` that governs both, in the coordinated-response form this registry already uses for compound obligations (SR-244's Requirement is one example). The miss is partly mine: 001's example wording invited the two-`shall` form that the lane then had to work around.\n\nOUTCOME: RETURN rows=1\n\n## Returns answered in the lane\n\n```toml\ntitle = \"WI-850 spine rework: SR-224's Requirement with both floor clauses under its one shall; re-sit SR-224\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nsr_refs = [\"SR-224\"]\nneeds = [\"WI-850\"]\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\nbuildtier = \"quick\"\n```\n\nThis is one cell of spine text. No code change and no record change are called for, because the perspective record stands.\n1. **SR-224's Requirement:** state both clauses under one `shall`, for example: \"The kit shall hold every skill description to the declared description floor: the descriptions it ships are at or above it, and its skills-index check fails, naming the skill, on any description shorter than it.\" The failing case becomes a response qualifier after the `shall`, which is its honest place here, since what the row obliges is the floor, not a fault-triggered reaction alone. The acceptance, the Rationale, LLR-281 and TC-291 stay as they are, and the record needs no rewrite because the row's subject and lens are unchanged.\n\nThen re-sit SR-224 for first approval, still as the Done-when's first act.\n\nSITTING: JUDGED kinds=first-approval\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":708,"cache_read_input_tokens":292472,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":708},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f031c6a5-ecf0-490f-9890-c7a8a87f92fc","uuid":"9def8f11-8570-4019-bde2-f665e605b735","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:59:22.342Z","request_id":"req_011CgJaSR7mxbaBxN59h9sgo","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014mVP2WRUWsWxWzD5BwfyUN":{"file_path":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-850\\docs\\reviews\\wi-850\\002-ADJUDICATE-ab4b6d8.md","content":"# WI-850 first-approval sitting at ab4b6d8\n\n## first-approval\n\nThis re-sits SR-224 after sitting 001 returned it. Commit ab4b6d8c2 answers both of 001's findings.\n\n**Finding (1), the perspective record, is closed.** `docs/plans/WI-850/sr-224.perspectives.toml` covers SR-224 under SN-005, and `hats.py record ... --check --strict` reports no findings when I run it. FIRST-RUN-ADOPTER produced the row, which matches its `Hat-Refs`, and every other applicable hat carries a no-finding I judge adequate. The row neither writes durable state, adds a rendered surface, nor touches an authority boundary, and TC-291 already holds both outcomes.\n\n**Finding (2) is answered in substance but not yet in form.** The Requirement now reads: \"The skill descriptions the kit ships are at or above the declared description floor, and